  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nShortwave energy over the Dakotas will lift into Canada this \nmorning, while surface low currently analyzed over eastern \nNebraska/western Iowa will continue to lift northeast through \nthe day today. The resultant loss of forcing will result in any\nlingering rain/snow in central parts of the state to taper off.\nHowever, secondary shortwave energy and a clipperesque surface \nlow passing from west to east along the International Border \nwill bring additional precipitation chances to the northwest \nmainly during the day today, and then to the far north this \nevening. This system is looking a bit cooler than it was \nyesterday which may produce snow or a rain/snow mix rather than \nrain itself. Either way, even if it snows, accumulations are \nunlikely. \n\nOther than precipitation chances, low-level stratus will persist\nthrough this morning before beginning to clear from southwest to\nnortheast this afternoon. As this stratus continues to sink\nearly this morning, widespread fog may develop. Just how dense\nit becomes and over how large of an area remains in question.\nThe elevated terrain over southwestern North Dakota seems the\nmost favored area for patchy dense fog. Though breezy winds at\nthe surface (west) and just off the surface (south central and \ninto the James River Valley) may keep it more low-level stratus \nwith minor visibility reductions. Will continue to monitor \nthrough the morning hours should any headlines be needed.\n\nA little light rain is possible Wednesday in the southeast, \nincluding the James River Valley. Otherwise, mostly dry\nconditions are expected Wednesday through Friday. Periodic\nprecipitation chances are then in the forecast Friday night,\nthrough this weekend, and into early next week. Based on the\ncurrent temperature trends, most of this precipitation is\nfavored to be in the form of rain, though some snow may mix in\nat times.\n\nSpeaking of temperatures, cooler conditions are on tap north and\neast today with highs in the 40s. However, highs in the 50s are\nexpected where skies clear earlier in the southwest. For the \nrest of this week, highs are favored to range from the mid 40s \nnorth to the mid 50s south, with some variance day to day. Lows \nwill be mostly in the 20s to low 30s, though may be a bit warmer\nthis weekend. All in all, these temperatures are near average \nto seasonably above average for this time of year.",
